# garak, for the in-app red-team sweep behind /garak/run and /garak/status.
#
# OFF by default — build with --build-arg INCLUDE_GARAK=true to get the sweep
# and its attack transcript. It costs image size: garak pulls torch and a large
# ML tree. Pulling torch from PyTorch's CPU index instead of PyPI keeps the
# CUDA runtime out — 13 nvidia-* packages plus triton, several GB of it — which
# the assistant has no use for, since it invokes garak with
# --model_type=openai against a remote endpoint and never loads a model
# locally.
#
# requirements-garak.txt is compiled from BOTH this file and requirements.in,
# as one complete set rather than an overlay, so when garak is wanted it
# replaces the base pins. Regenerate with uv, for linux/amd64:
#
#   uv pip compile --python-platform x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu \
#     --python-version 3.11 --index-strategy unsafe-best-match \
#     -o requirements-garak.txt requirements.in requirements-garak.in
#
# It must be uv, not pip-compile. pip-compile backtracks through every release
# of every LLM SDK garak supports and does not terminate — a run was killed at
# three hours, still descending through anthropic 0.112. uv answers in under a
# minute, and reports a real conflict as a conflict.
#
# unsafe-best-match is needed because the PyTorch CPU index below mirrors an
# old tqdm that shadows PyPI's newer one, which garak requires. uv defaults to
# first-index-wins to resist dependency confusion; both indexes here are
# first-party, so letting it pick the best version across them is safe.
--extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu

garak>=0.15
